"main.py": {
  role: "CLI entry point (CLIUniApp)",
  summary: `Starts the command-line app. Creates ONE Database, two controllers and two menus, then loops on the University System menu: A = admin, S = student, X = exit.`,
  why: `Keeps "wiring" (creating objects and connecting them) in one place. The menus and controllers do the real work.`,
  say: `main.py is the starting point of the CLI. It creates one Database object for students.data and passes that same object to both controllers. Then it shows the University System menu in a loop. A opens the admin menu, S opens the student menu, X prints "Thank You" and stops.`,
  notes: [
    [1, 1, "from database.database import Database", `Imports the Database class from the file database/database.py.`, `main() needs it to open students.data.`, {d: `database/database.py`}],
    [3, 4, "AdminController", `Imports the two controller classes: AdminController (admin work) and StudentController (login and register).`, `main() creates one of each.`],
    [6, 7, "StudentMenu", `Imports the two menu classes that print menus and read input.`, `Menus are the "front", controllers are the "back".`],
    [9, 9, "MainInputConstants", `Imports the menu letters A, S and X from utils/constants.py.`, `Using named constants instead of typing "A" everywhere makes the code easier to read and change.`],
    [12, 12, "def main", `Defines the function main(). Everything the CLI does starts here.`, `Putting the code in a function lets tests call main.main() and lets the file be imported without running.`],
    [14, 14, "Database(filename=", `Creates the Database object for the file students.data. If the file does not exist it is created with "[]". All students are loaded into memory.`, `Only ONE database object exists, so every part of the app sees the same data.`, {o: `database object`, s: `This line opens students.data once. The same object is shared with both controllers, so they always see the same list of students.`}],
    [17, 18, "StudentController(database)", `Creates the two controllers and gives each one the same database object. This is called dependency injection: the object is passed in, not created inside.`, `Lets tests replace the database with a fake (the tests in tests/test_main.py do this with patch).`],
    [21, 22, "StudentMenu(student_controller)", `Creates the menus and gives each menu its controller.`, `Separates "talking to the user" (menu) from "doing the work" (controller).`],
    [25, 25, "while True", `Starts a loop that never ends by itself. It only stops at the "break" on line 37.`, `The menu must keep appearing until the user types X.`],
    [26, 26, "input(", `Shows the prompt "University System: (A)dmin, (S)tudent, or X : " and waits for the user to type.`, `This is the top-level menu from the assignment sample.`, {i: `text typed by the user`, o: `user_input (a string)`}],
    [27, 29, "#right now typing", `Comments explaining why the next line was added: tutors may type lowercase letters.`, `Comments are ignored by Python.`],
    [30, 30, ".strip().upper()", `Removes spaces at the start and end (strip) and changes letters to capitals (upper). So " s " becomes "S".`, `Makes the main menu forgiving: a, s, x and extra spaces all work.`, {s: `I clean the input first, so lowercase and extra spaces still work. That is why typing " s " opens the student menu.`, w: `Only the MAIN menu does this. The student menu (l/r/x) and admin menu (c/g/p/r/s/x) do NOT strip or change case, so "L" or " l" is "Invalid input" there.`}],
    [31, 32, "INPUT_STUDENT_SYSTEM", `If the input is "S", run the student menu. When that menu exits, we come back to this loop.`, `Routes the user to the student system.`],
    [33, 34, "INPUT_ADMIN_SYSTEM", `If the input is "A", run the admin menu.`, `Routes the user to the admin system.`],
    [35, 37, "INPUT_EXIT", `If the input is "X", print "Thank You" and break out of the loop, so main() ends and the program stops.`, `This is the only way to leave the program normally.`, {o: `prints: Thank You`}],
    [38, 39, "else", `Any other input prints "Invalid input" and the loop asks again.`, `Handles wrong input without crashing.`],
    [42, 43, "__name__", `Runs main() only when this file is started directly (python3 main.py). When a test imports main.py, main() does not run automatically.`, `Standard Python pattern. tests/test_main.py relies on it.`, {s: `This guard means the program only starts when I run the file directly, not when it is imported by a test.`}],
  ],
},

