"utils/constants.py": {
  role: "Shared constants",
  summary: `Groups fixed values into small classes: menu letters, grade thresholds, pass mark, regex patterns, ID ranges, subject limits.`,
  why: `No "magic numbers" scattered in the code. Change a value once, and every file that uses it changes.`,
  say: `constants.py keeps all fixed values in one place, like the menu letters, the grade bands and the email and password patterns. The controllers import them so the rules are not repeated.`,
  notes: [
    [2, 5, "class MainInputConstants", `Letters for the top menu: A (admin), S (student), X (exit). Capital letters because main.py upper-cases the input.`, `Used by main.py.`],
    [7, 10, "class StudentMenuInputConstants", `Letters for the student menu: l (login), r (register), x (exit). Lowercase.`, `Used by student_system/student_menu.py.`],
    [12, 18, "class AdminMenuInputConstants", `Letters for the admin menu: c clear, g group, p partition, r remove, s show, x exit.`, `Used by admin_system/admin_menu.py.`],
    [21, 32, "class MarkThresholdConstants", `Grade bands. Each number is the UPPER limit (not included) of a band: below 50 = Z, below 65 = P, below 75 = C, below 85 = D, otherwise HD. HD_NUMBER = 100 is defined but the code uses "else" for HD.`, `One grading table shared by models/subject.py and the admin controller.`, {s: `Each number is the top of a band. A mark of 65 is not below 65, so it is a C, not a P.`}],
    [34, 35, "class PassThresholdConstants", `PASS = 50. An average of 50 or more is PASS, below 50 is FAIL.`, `Used by partition_student().`],
    [37, 40, "class StudentValidationConstants", `Two regular expressions (regex). EMAIL_PATTERN: letters, a dot, letters, then exactly @university.com. PASSWORD_PATTERN: one capital letter, then 4 or more letters, then 3 or more digits, nothing else.`, `Used by StudentController (login/register) and EnrolmentMenu (change password).`, {s: `The email must look like firstname.lastname@university.com. The password must start with a capital, have at least 5 letters in total, then at least 3 digits, like Helloworld123.`}],
    [38, 38, "EMAIL_PATTERN", `^ = start, [a-zA-Z]+ = one or more letters, \\. = a real dot, [a-zA-Z]+ = letters, @university\\.com = the exact domain, $ = end.`, `Numbers or extra dots in the name are NOT allowed, e.g. john.smith2@university.com fails.`],
    [40, 40, "PASSWORD_PATTERN", `^[A-Z] = first letter is a capital. [a-zA-Z]{4,} = 4 or more letters after it (so 5+ letters in total). [0-9]{3,} = 3 or more digits. $ = nothing after the digits.`, `Matches the requirement "starts with uppercase, 5+ letters, followed by 3+ digits". Password123 passes; password123, Pass12, Pass123 (only 4 letters) fail.`],
    [42, 45, "class StudentIdConstants", `Student ID range 1 to 999999, shown with 6 digits (e.g. 000042).`, `Used by generate_unique_id() in StudentController.`],
    [47, 52, "class EnrolmentMenuInputConstants", `Letters for the student course menu: c change password, e enrol, r remove, s show, x exit.`, `Used by student_system/enrolment_menu.py.`],
    [54, 60, "class SubjectConstants", `Subject ID range 1-999 (3 digits), marks 25-100, max 4 subjects.`, `Intended as the single source of these values.`, {w: `NOT used anywhere. models/subject.py defines its own copies (BIGGEST_ID, SMALLEST_MARK...) and enrolment_controller.py defines MAX_SUBJECTS = 4 again. Same values, so behaviour is correct, but the rule is written in three places. A good "what would you improve?" answer.`}],
  ],
},

